63636363636,\\n  \\\"created_epoch\\\": 0,\\n  \\\"sources\\\": [],\\n  \\\"merged_from\\\": [],\\n  \\\"origin\\\": \\\"c000006\\\",\\n  \\\"last_evaluated_epoch\\\": 3,\\n  \\\"report_ids\\\": [\\n   \\\"R838be7703a\\\"\\n  ]\\n },\\n {\\n  \\\"id\\\": \\\"K1038\\\",\\n  \\\"text\\\": \\\"SGD vs adaptive,SGD calibrated Delta>=3x rival,rivalDelta<.1: deep(>=3) ZERO-LN smooth MLPs are task-dependent, not automatically frozen. Explicit plain-MSE/CE base-lab: deep SGD wins11/30 in11sets,loss ratio1.056; plain3/15(1.897),residual8/15(.991). Regression5/21; spiral4/5; XOR2/4: do NOT apply a universal penalty. Almost all use m=.9; budgets4096/8192samples; no deep rectifier cases. With >=1LN,SGD wins413/471(ratio.578). Older broader21/60 and493/563 use different filters. Keep residual,task,activation and budget scope; loss ratios do not measure parameter motion. Calibration:K1019.\\\",\\n  \\\"support_count\\\": 0.75,\\n  \\\"failure_count\\\": 0.25,\\n  \\\"credibility\\\": 0.5833333333333334,\\n  \\\"created_epoch\\\": 0,\\n  \\\"sources\\\": [],\\n  \\\"merged_from\\\": [],\\n  \\\"origin\\\": \\\"c000104\\\",\\n  \\\"mechanism\\\": \\\"Held-out loss and win rates do not measure parameter displacement or freezing. Gradient attenuation remains a hypothesis; residual/task-specific ranking differences are empirical.\\\",\\n  \\\"regime\\\": {\\n   \\\"family\\\": [\\n    \\\"univariate_regression\\\",\\n    \\\"multivariate_regression\\\",\\n    \\\"spiral_classification\\\",\\n    \\\"xor_classification\\\"\\n   ],\\n   \\\"model_type\\\": [\\n    \\\"mlp\\\"\\n   ],\\n   \\\"optimizer\\\": [\\n    \\\"SGD\\\"\\n   ],\\n   \\\"depth\\\": [\\n    3,\\n    5\\n   ],\\n   \\\"n_ln\\\": [\\n    0,\\n    0\\n   ],\\n   \\\"activation\\\": [\\n    \\\"silu\\\",\\n    \\\"gelu\\\"\\n   ],\\n   \\\"budget\\\": [\\n    4096,\\n    8192\\n   ],\\n   \\\"note\\\": \\\"SGD Delta>=3x adaptive,rival<.1; mostly m.9,mixed architectures.\\\"\\n  },\\n  \\\"phase\\\": \\\"Explicit base plain-loss audit: deep smooth zeroLN SGD11/30,11sets,ratio1.056; plain3/15,1.897;residual8/15,.991;regression5/21;spiral4/5;XOR2/4. Normalized413/471,.578. Older21/60,18sets and493/563 use different filters. No deep rectifier cases or16k/32k budgets.\\\",\\n  \\\"refines\\\": \\\"K1019\\\",\\n  \\\"last_evaluated_epoch\\\": 1,\\n  \\\"report_ids\\\": [\\n   \\\"R04f88f629b\\\",\\n   \\\"R838be7703a\\\"\\n  ]\\n }\\n]\\n\\n# R04f88f629b: Condition-first architecture claims: validated counts and counterexamples (0c676d9d5b42ccf0b715c62f8996ab9f0c124039)\\n# Condition-First Repair of Architecture Claims\\n\\n## Question and Sources\\n\\nInvestigated negative-utility K1006 (-0.07 n33), K1018 (-0.06 n52), K1009 (-0.06 n22), and K1038 (-0.03 n68), distinguishing retrieval/use mis-scope from factual failure. Independently validated `q_1f04dc` without requiring a causal mechanism.\\n\\nFrozen inputs: 863 history records and 11,215 base-lab candidates. A checkpoint refresh retained epoch-1 history and exposed current claim repairs plus experimental results. Base backtests exclude experiments; controlled results are reported separately.\\n\\nThe supplied utility estimates cannot be reconstructed from visible citations: K1006/K1018/K1009/K1038 appear in 16/3/1/1 cited records, respectively. Complete retrieval exposure and counterfactual utilities are unavailable. Negative utility therefore signals use risk, not necessarily false architecture facts.\\n\\nLab comparisons use held-out mean losses within one `set_id`. Excluded candidates are removed; nonexcluded partial-seed failures are retained for denominator reconstruction and separately tested. History supplies ordinal gold, not candidate loss magnitudes.\\n\\n## Definitions and Methods\\n\\nDepth counts hidden MLP blocks between projection and head. LN count refers to normalized hidden blocks. Residual addition is not defined as an LN-equivalent optimization unit.\\n\\nCalibrated Δ follows K1001/K1019: Adam/AdamW/RMSprop `lr*T`; Adagrad `2*lr*sqrt(T)`; SGD `lr*T*g/(1-m)`, using `g=.03` for momentum MSE regression and `.01` otherwise. This is a proxy, not measured parameter motion.\\n\\nThe scoped lab comparison requires plain MSE, a nonresidual candidate with exactly one additional LN versus a residual rival, Δ ratio 0.5–2, and the same set. Both Δ must be below 0.1 for the low regime. Equal width, depth, activation, or optimizer configuration is not required by the reported pooled fact.\\n\\nHistory backtesting requires equal optimizer type/lr and low Δ. Ranked gold supplies eligible pair order; single-winner gold supplies order only when the winner belongs to the pair. The focal question is excluded from the historical backtest.\\n\\n## Validated LN-versus-Residual Fact\\n\\nThe reported lab result reproduces exactly:\\n\\n| Comparison | Plain higher-LN wins | Sets | Median MSE ratio |\\n|---|---:|---:|---:|\\n| Multivariate, both Δ<0.1 | 23/31 | 25 | 0.906336 |\\n| Multivariate, at least one Δ≥0.1 | 5/13 | 12 | 1.054061 |\\n| Univariate, both Δ<0.1 | 17/34 | 25 | 0.988537 |\\n\\nThe historical result also reproduces: excluding `q_1f04dc`, 40/52 wins across 31 questions; Adagrad 12/17 across nine questions. Including the focal case gives 41/53 and Adagrad 13/18. Thus the focal exclusion explains the denominator difference.\\n\\nIn `q_1f04dc`, A is plain depth2 width192 SiLU with two LN; B is residual depth1 width192 LeakyReLU with one LN. Both use Adagrad .001, decay .001, 256 steps, batch64, 16,384 samples, Δ=.032. Gold A contradicts the solver’s residual=one-LN tie followed by a rectifier tie-break. This directly demonstrates scoring misuse, not universal residual inferiority.\\n\\n## Stratification and Counterexamples\\n\\nLow multivariate lab evidence is concentrated at 4,096 samples: 22/30 wins, ratio .908. The remaining 8,192-sample pair wins. No eligible pair directly matches the focal 16,384-sample budget.\\n\\nEqual widths give 14/19 wins, ratio .889; plain-narrower comparisons 9/12, .924. No plain-wider comparisons exist. Input dimensions 2/3/5/8 give 3/3, 9/13, 8/11, and 3/4 wins.\\n\\nPlain-minus-residual depth gaps −1/0/+1/+2/+3 give 5/6, 7/8, 4/9, 2/2, and 5/6 wins. Crucially, the focal +1-depth subset has median ratio 1.166, opposing a depth-specific universal preference.\\n\\nEqual optimizer type/lr yields only 4/6 wins, ratio .913. No pair matches the full optimizer configuration. Equal-type AdamW gives 0/2, RMSprop 2/2, SGD 3/3. Mixed Adagrad-plain versus SGD-residual gives 1/4, ratio 1.119. These small, confounded strata do not establish optimizer-specific laws.\\n\\nRemoving every candidate with any failed seed changes low evidence to 22/30, ratio .898, and higher-Δ evidence to 3/10, ratio 1.076. Exact 23/31 requires retaining a nonexcluded partial-failure comparison.\\n\\nA 10,000-resample set-cluster bootstrap gives descriptive 95% ranges of .567–.903 for win fraction and .721–.959 for median ratio. These are sampling-sensitivity intervals, not causal guarantees.\\n\\nAll eight low-Δ losses remain recorded. Examples include `mvar_086f22` and `mvar_cf5f29`: plain depth5 SiLU three-LN loses to residual depth4 LeakyReLU two-LN at equal width256, with ratios 1.193 and 1.379. Optimizer decay differs. These counterexamples are not explained away.\\n\\n## Visible Retrieval/Use Records\\n\\nK1006 has direct scoring failures in `q_1f04dc` and conflicting residual/LN outcomes in `q_ca0dea`. Spiral and univariate records also challenge universal additive scoring. Their post-hoc mechanistic explanations are not treated as measurements.\\n\\nK1018 succeeds in `q_09e52b`: sixteen-feature XOR, Adam Δ=.0768, smooth/fewer-LN winner. But `q_917f30` records a controlled counterexample near Δ=.0512. Low-dimensional `q_a504f7` concerns residual overcredit outside the high-dimensional reversal condition.\\n\\nK1009’s sole visible citation, `q_b0ae9d`, concerns low-Δ tabular ranking. Its missed comparison confounds width, activation, LN placement, and depth. It does not falsify the high-Δ aggregate.\\n\\nK1038’s sole visible citation, `q_0e3752`, says the claim helped. The error concerned activation-versus-depth precedence in normalized residual regression, not zero-LN SGD. Relative held-out loss cannot demonstrate freezing.\\n\\n## Independent Scope Audit\\n\\nA separate base-lab audit uses plain MSE/CE and explicit same-set filters. XOR/tabular LN pairs match optimizer type/lr but leave architecture and remaining optimizer settings confounded.\\n\\nFor XOR with ≥8 features, more-LN wins 14/32 below Δ=.05, ratio 1.001, versus 36/51 above it, ratio .891. No base high-Δ pair matches width, depth, activation, and residual simultaneously. The older 8/32 high and 33/41 low counts have an underidentified selection filter and do not reproduce under this explicit audit.\\n\\nHigh-Δ XOR more-LN outcomes vary by optimizer: Adam8/14, AdamW9/12, RMSprop10/14, Adagrad1/1, SGD8/10. Samples4096/8192 give 34/47 and 2/4. These observations do not establish an opposite universal LN rule.\\n\\nTabular high-Δ more-LN outcomes differ by data rule: piecewise9/17, ratio .987; smooth-additive5/13, 1.196; sparse-interaction0/10, 1.495. Below .1, corresponding wins are 38/56, 24/40, and 29/45. The pooled reversal does not describe piecewise boundaries reliably. High evidence covers 4,096/8,192 samples, with width/depth/activation/decay still confounded.\\n\\nFor SGD Δ≥3× an adaptive rival with rival Δ<.1, normalized SGD wins413/471, ratio .578; zero-LN68/119, .983; deep zero-LN11/30, 1.056. Deep plain SGD wins3/15, ratio1.897, versus residual8/15, .991. Regression gives5/21, spiral4/5, XOR2/4. Almost all deep cases use momentum .9; none are rectifiers. Width and sample-budget strata vary substantially. Older 21/60 and493/563 counts use different filters and must not share these denominators.\\n\\n## Preregistered Controlled Tests\\n\\nPredictions were saved before experiments. Only allowed lab datasets were used.\\n\\nOn `mvar_e57563`, fixed width96 depth1 GELU, Adagrad .001, decay0, 256 steps, batch16, Δ=.032, residual and LN were crossed:\\n\\n| Architecture | Mean MSE | Seed SD |\\n|---|---:|---:|\\n| Plain, LN0 | .459344 | .007793 |\\n| Residual, LN0 | .451954 | .011002 |\\n| Plain, LN1 | .420702 | .010103 |\\n| Residual, LN1 | .419177 | .007300 |\\n\\nThe predicted plain-LN1 versus residual-LN0 preference holds: ratio .931, approximately 6.9% lower MSE. Equal-LN residual benefits are small. This supports one scoped comparison, not transfer to the focal target, depth, activation, or budget.\\n\\nOn sixteen-feature `xorcls_0dd7d2`, fixed width24 depth2 plain GELU AdamW, 256 steps and batch16, compare one versus two LN:\\n\\n| Δ | One-LN CE | Two-LN CE |\\n|---|---:|---:|\\n| .0256 | .692235 | .692118 |\\n| .0512 | .687533 | .684777 |\\n| .0768 | .681533 | .676190 |\\n\\nMore-LN wins all three means. The preregistered high-Δ fewer-LN prediction fails. The high-Δ ratio is .992; differences are small relative to seed SDs, and paired significance cannot be computed without seed-level covariance. No tested condition shows a sharp switch.\\n\\nCompeting explanations include architecture/optimizer selection, target-specific effects, and imperfect Δ calibration. The fixed XOR threshold prediction was tested and failed here. SGD freezing requires trajectories or initial-to-final measurements, which these rankings do not provide.\\n\\n## Repairs and Boundaries\\n\\nFour short, condition-first revisions were submitted for K1006/K1018/K1009/K1038. They remove residual=one-LN scoring, an automatic XOR LN switch, pooled tabular precedence, and “near-frozen” SGD wording while retaining scoped counts, effects, and counterexamples.\\n\\nRefresh exposed K1076, but targeting it was rejected by the proposal snapshot. Its validated definitions and limitations were instead incorporated into K1006 without creating a duplicate claim.\\n\\nSolver precedence should first check family/data, loss, optimizer/calibrated Δ, actual steps/sample budget, and comparable architecture. Scoped preferences must not override stronger evidence outside these gates. A counterexample limits a rule; it need not establish the opposite universal rule.\\n\\nUnresolved questions include exact replication of the focal 16,384-sample Adagrad configuration, matched LN-placement comparisons, transfer across targets, and SGD parameter trajectories. No causal theory is claimed.\\n\\n## Reproducibility and Publication\\n\\nSaved code, source records, candidate pairs, strata, predictions, controlled results, bootstrap specification, and accepted operations accompany `report.md`. Scripts compile and execute through the allowed lab Python interface.\\n\\nPublished report `R04f88f629b`; final repository commit `947b7c2b371af9f9267eaf4360f26857f5b88338`. Both controlled experiments completed.\\n\\n# R838be7703a: Plain versus momentum SGD: effective-rate parity audit (f35df9773407e9781365120f0e8cd02fd3e16997)\\n# Plain versus Momentum SGD at Effective-Rate Parity\\n\\n## Scope and initial audit\\nQuestion q_0a12ba incorrectly transferred K1019's SGD-versus-adaptive gradient-scale calibration into a plain-versus-momentum comparison. A uses SGD lr.0001/m.9/wd0; D uses plain lr.001/wd1e-4. Same default-init nonresidual depth3 width32 SiLU MLP, LN [T,T,T], plain MSE, T1024 batch32. Gold B<C<E<D<A; only A/D were inverted. This is an ordinal result with decay confounding, not a measured causal explanation.\\n\\nRead current K1003/K1019/K1038 and persisted science reports. Preserve surviving repairs and K1019's established cross-family calibration; no revisions planned. Equal asymptotic SGD rate here is lr/(1-m)=.001 on both sides, with no adaptive-family gradient multiplier.\\n\\nHistorical q_21e9b3/q_974d7a/q_9a31dd/q_8e7c7f reproduce plain4/4 at T256, but none share decay. q_9a31dd plain decay.001 violates <=1e-4; q_8e7c7f has zero LN. These are not four normalized low-decay full-config controls. Base-lab model-matched comparisons reproduce plain3/3 across2sets: two tabular comparisons share a plain candidate and differ in decay; one GRU pair shares decay. Rates are .0003/.00003 (tabular) and .003/.0003 (GRU), not .001/.0001. Shared model/loss/budget is not shared full configuration.\\n\\n## Preregistered tests, before new outcomes\\nUse allowed mvar_e57563 and mvar_5a829e. Fix default-init nonresidual depth3 width32 SiLU MLP, pre-linear LN [T,T,T], output1, each dataset's input dimension; plain mse, batch32. Cross T256/T1024 and shared SGD weight decay0/1e-4. For each cell compare lr.001/m0 versus lr.0001/m.9. All other optimizer knobs use runner defaults. Predict plain lower held-out mean MSE in all eight pairs; predict no robust large advantage, with startup-only advantage tending to diminish at T1024. Count every miss, failure and unavailable measurement. No candidate outcome filtering. Same-model convention does not prove identical seed weights/batch streams without runner evidence.\\n\\nUnder fixed gradients and conventional zero-velocity momentum, cumulative displacement ratio is 1-9*(1-.9**T)/T: .96484375 at256 and .99121094 at1024. This calculation is not proof of held-out behavior; changing gradients, noise filtering, curvature, regularization and generalization are competing explanations. Endpoint MSE cannot distinguish them.\\n\\n## Reproducibility and uncertainty\\nFrozen source history/KB/lab snapshots and parity pairs saved under research/. Base comparisons are within set only; experiments remain separate. Means/SD are ten-seed summaries, not paired significance or future-dataset reliability. Numerical outcomes pending. Report is usable but unfinished; at most one narrow new claim after evidence, no replacement of K1019.\\n\"}],\"isError\":false}")
